  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 314 AM EDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nConvective complex over far ne IL and nw OH should exit the area to \nthe southeast by 9-10z. An isolated strong-severe cell (wind) \nremains possible, though activity likely to remain largely outflow \ndominant in our area. Lower chances (10-30%) for a few showers and \nstorms linger during the daylight hours today as the primary \nfrontal zone shifts south through the area. Another humid day \notherwise along/south of US 30 in IN where peak PM heat indices \nare expected to top out in the 90s. \n\nCanadian high pressure builds in from the northeast by Saturday\nafternoon ushering in cooler and less humid air into the area. \nMeanwhile, the upper-level ridge is displaced well over the \nCentral and Northern Plains. High pressure remains in place \nthrough Monday, but the upepr-level ridge is expanding back \neastward overhead by this time. This occurs as a trough moves \nwell inland to the Pacific Northwest. Temperatures warm well \ninto the 80s Monday but dry weather looks to prevail with a lack\nof forcing mechanism. \n\nMidweek, low pressure occludes Manitoba/Ontario while a trailing \nfrontal boundary spills into the Midwest. This represents our best \nchance for rain and storms in the forecast period, but not quite a \nslam-dunk given poor moisture transport.  The upper-level ridge \nstill lingers over the Southern Plains and looks to expand overhead \nlate in the week.",
